I kind of take the train everywhere I need to go.

42
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.900
But even my car back in the United States

43
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.900
has the option to create a Wi-Fi hotspot

44
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.900
where you'll use the cellular subscription

45
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.900
that you pay your manufacturer for

46
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.900
to actually create a Wi-Fi network local to your vehicle

47
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.900
so that when you're driving across town,

48
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.900
your kids want to watch Netflix on their iPads,

49
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.900
they can do that, right?

50
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.900
And that kind of user-facing convenience technology

51
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.900
is really the driving force behind a lot of these advancements

52
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.900
in vehicle networking and connectivity.

53
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.900
Of course, it also includes things like self-driving cars,

54
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.900
which are a huge industry of their own, right?

55
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.900
And now you have self-driving cars that have no drivers inside them.

56
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.900
You have the robo-taxis.

57
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.900
You can see them in different parts of the United States

58
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.900
and very soon here in Japan, actually.

59
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.900
And all of these additions of technology to a vehicle

60
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.900
turn it into something that what used to be like a big metal cage,

61
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.900
which is wheels on an engine,

62
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.900
is now a bunch of computers on, like I said, wheels.

63
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.900
And this complexity introduced

64
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.900
and the amount of software that's running inside these vehicles

65
